@page "/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(DistributedTestingWithAspireAndPlaywrightInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                An API can return success while a user still cannot complete the workflow.
                <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Testing</Highlight> starts the real AppHost and its
                resources for a test; <Highlight>Playwright</Highlight> drives a browser through
                the application UI and checks the result a user sees.
            </p>
            <p>
                This walkthrough applies that pattern to the
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet/">existing multi-service topology</a>:
                one shared fixture owns startup and cleanup, then a browser test performs an
                existing business flow and verifies its persisted result in the UI.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                A service-level functional test can exercise an API pipeline in-process, for
                example with <Highlight>WebApplicationFactory</Highlight>; it can also use a real
                database when configured to do so. These focused tests are fast and give precise
                failure signals, but they do not automatically cross a real browser, identity
                boundary, service discovery, and all production service processes.
            </p>
            <p>
                An Aspire-backed browser test runs those services as separate processes and follows
                the actual UI-to-service-to-database path. It costs more time and container
                resources, but can reveal broken login redirects, endpoint wiring, role-specific
                screens, or a persisted result that never appears after navigation. Keep both
                scopes: use focused functional tests for service behavior and Playwright for
                critical end-to-end user journeys.
            </p>
            <p>
                Health checks only establish resource readiness; a browser assertion establishes
                whether the user-visible workflow works. We will seed isolated test prerequisites,
                perform an existing order-entry flow through accessible UI controls, and verify
                that its unique result remains visible after a fresh page load.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>1. Reference the existing AppHost from your test project</h4>
            <p>
                Add a distributed-test project to the existing solution and reference its AppHost.
                Use the solution's current xUnit v3 setup together with
                <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Testing</Highlight> and Playwright's xUnit v3 base
                classes. Replace the generated AppHost type and resource names below with those in
                your solution; this article does not add a separate demo application.
            </p>

            <h4>2. Share one bounded AppHost fixture across the test assembly</h4>
            <p>
                An assembly fixture starts the production-shaped graph once, waits for resources
                and business-facing probes, then disposes the graph when the suite ends. A linked
                cancellation token bounds startup and carries test-run cancellation through each
                wait and request. The following ordered blocks are consecutive parts of the same
                fixture file; retaining the builder field is important because startup can fail
                before an AppHost instance exists.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Aspire.Hosting;
using Aspire.Hosting.Testing;
using Xunit;

[assembly: AssemblyFixture(typeof(AppFixture))]

public sealed class AppFixture : IAsyncLifetime
{
    private static readonly TimeSpan StartupLimit =
        TimeSpan.FromSeconds(120);
    private static readonly TimeSpan ShutdownLimit =
        TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30);
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _shutdown = new();
    private IDistributedApplicationTestingBuilder? _builder;
    private DistributedApplication? _app;
    private bool _disposed;

    public DistributedApplication App =>
        _app ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("AppHost is not ready.");
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Initialize the AppHost and wait for its required resources and probes:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    public async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        var testCancellation = TestContext.Current.CancellationToken;
        using var startup = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
            _shutdown.Token, testCancellation);
        startup.CancelAfter(StartupLimit);
        try
        {
            _builder = await DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder
                .CreateAsync&lt;Projects.AppHost&gt;(startup.Token);
            _app = await _builder.BuildAsync(startup.Token);
            await _app.StartAsync(startup.Token);
            await WaitForRequiredResourcesAndProbesAsync(startup.Token);
        }
        catch (Exception startupError)
        {
            try
            {
                await DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (Exception cleanupError)
            {
                throw new AggregateException(
                    "AppHost startup and cleanup both failed.",
                    startupError, cleanupError);
            }
            throw;
        }
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>The health and HTTP probes use the same linked startup deadline:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    private async Task WaitForRequiredResourcesAndProbesAsync(
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        await App.ResourceNotifications.WaitForResourceHealthyAsync(
            "landing", cancellationToken);
        using var identity = App.CreateHttpClient("identity");
        await WaitForSuccessAsync(
            identity, "/.well-known/openid-configuration", cancellationToken);
        using var api = App.CreateHttpClient("api");
        await WaitForSuccessAsync(api, "/api/ping", cancellationToken);
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Keep the bounded probe helper in the same fixture file:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    private static async Task WaitForSuccessAsync(
        HttpClient client, string path, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        while (true)
        {
            try
            {
                using var response = await client.GetAsync(path, cancellationToken);
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
                return;
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException ex) when (
                !cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested &&
                (ex.StatusCode is null || (int)ex.StatusCode.Value >= 500))
            {
                await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500), cancellationToken);
            }
        }
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Finally, dispose the partially or fully started host with a bounded stop:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_disposed) return;
        _disposed = true;
        _shutdown.Cancel();
        using var shutdown = new CancellationTokenSource(ShutdownLimit);
        try
        {
            if (_app is not null)
            {
                try { await _app.StopAsync(shutdown.Token); }
                finally { await _app.DisposeAsync(); }
            }
            else if (_builder is not null)
            {
                await _builder.DisposeAsync();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _shutdown.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The resource notification waits for the landing service's health check. The
                identity discovery document and API ping then verify useful HTTP contracts before
                the fixture exposes the browser endpoint. In your app, use its existing health
                routes and a small unauthenticated readiness probe where one is available; give
                each wait and probe the same startup deadline.
            </p>
            <p>
                The failure path attempts awaited cleanup of anything already created, and xUnit
                later disposes the assembly fixture after the suite. Normal disposal stops the
                AppHost and awaits cleanup of its managed test resources. This keeps test writes
                out of shared staging only when the graph targets its own disposable database;
                Aspire does not enforce that boundary. Treat "fire and forget" as a disposable
                test run whose startup and cleanup are still awaited—not detached work. Teardown
                cannot clean an external database or persistent volume, and cannot run after
                forced termination.
            </p>
            <p>
                Aspire's test builder assigns endpoints at runtime. Always retrieve the URL with
                <Highlight>GetEndpoint</Highlight> or create a resource-specific
                <Highlight>HttpClient</Highlight>; do not bake a port number into the test.
                Random ports do not isolate explicitly shared databases, volumes, or other
                external dependencies. Before running browser tests, trust the local ASP.NET
                development certificate on supported desktop platforms with
                <Highlight>dotnet dev-certs https --trust</Highlight>. On platforms where that
                command does not install trust for the browser, add the certificate to the
                browser/OS trust store or set <Highlight>IgnoreHTTPSErrors</Highlight> only on
                the isolated Playwright context; never disable certificate validation globally.
            </p>

            <h4>3. Inject the fixture into a Playwright PageTest</h4>
            <p>
                Playwright's <Highlight>PageTest</Highlight> provides an isolated browser context
                for each test. xUnit injects the assembly fixture through the constructor, so
                tests share the running graph but not browser cookies or local storage. This
                example's custom base class sets bounded timeouts and enables video before the
                context is created; it saves the recording after closing the context and still
                awaits base-class disposal if saving fails.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3;

public abstract class DistributedPageTest : PageTest
{
    private IVideo? _video;

    public override BrowserNewContextOptions ContextOptions()
    {
        var directory = Path.Combine("artifacts", "videos");
        Directory.CreateDirectory(directory);
        var options = base.ContextOptions();
        options.RecordVideoDir = directory;
        return options;
    }

    public override async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        await base.InitializeAsync();
        Assertions.SetDefaultExpectTimeout(30_000);
        Context.SetDefaultTimeout(60_000);
        Context.SetDefaultNavigationTimeout(60_000);
        _video = Page.Video;
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Add this disposal override to that base class:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    public override async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (_video is not null)
            {
                await Context.CloseAsync();
                var target = Path.Combine(
                    "artifacts", "videos", $"{Guid.NewGuid():N}.webm");
                await _video.SaveAsAsync(target);
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            await base.DisposeAsync();
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The representative real workflow has an administrator create an isolated client
                and branch plus owner/operator users. The owner configures settings and creates
                category, item, supplier, and purchase prerequisites; the operator creates a
                customer, saves a draft, edits it, and finalizes the order. The Orders table shows
                the result before and after reload, exercising persistence through the web, API,
                and database processes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The following is an illustrative test excerpt, not a standalone test application.
                Its page-object calls are application-specific Playwright code, not Aspire or
                Playwright APIs. Map them to existing screens and accessible labels; obtain test
                identities from secure test configuration, never hard-code credentials.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Xunit;

public sealed class E2ETests(AppFixture fixture) : DistributedPageTest
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Operator_finalizes_order_and_sees_it_in_orders()
    {
        var token = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
        var orderName = $"Test order {token}";
        var webUrl = fixture.App.GetEndpoint("web", "https");
        await Page.GotoAsync(webUrl.ToString());
        try
        {
            await CreateBranchScenarioThroughUiAsync(Page, token);
            await CreateAndFinalizeOrderThroughUiAsync(Page, orderName);
            var row = Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Row)
                .Filter(new() { HasText = orderName });
            await Expect(row).ToBeVisibleAsync();
            await Page.ReloadAsync();
            await Expect(row).ToBeVisibleAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            await RemoveScenarioThroughUiAsync(Page, token);
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The full GUID suffix gives each case unique client, branch, user, and order data
                while the assembly fixture shares one database across tests. UI cleanup is
                awaited from the test's <Highlight>finally</Highlight> path; if the UI is no
                longer available, fixture disposal remains the separate cleanup boundary for
                containers and databases. Other owner cases cover branch features and room
                management. Use retrying Playwright assertions for eventual UI state instead of
                fixed delays or <Highlight>NetworkIdle</Highlight> as a readiness guarantee.
            </p>

            <h4>4. Run the existing suite against its own resources</h4>
            <p>
                Replace the project path with the existing distributed-test project. These
                PowerShell commands build it, install the matching Chromium browser through the
                generated Playwright script, then run the suite. The example output path assumes
                the project targets <Highlight>net11.0</Highlight>; use its actual target framework
                if different. Run each line in PowerShell and proceed only after it exits with
                code zero; a passing test run reports zero failures.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
$project = "path\to\DistributedTests\DistributedTests.csproj"
dotnet build $project
& "path\to\DistributedTests\bin\Debug\net11.0\playwright.ps1" install chromium
dotnet test $project
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Each test host begins with the application's normal schema and seed baseline, but
                the assembly fixture shares that database across the suite. Tests should use
                unique records because xUnit may run methods in parallel, and must not assume a
                reset between methods. At normal suite
                teardown, disposing the fixture removes its managed database and services. This
                only prevents accumulation in shared staging if the test graph actually targets
                its own local database; never point the fixture at production or a shared
                persistent test database.
            </p>

            <h4>5. Extend the same test boundary across repositories</h4>
            <p>
                An enterprise test graph can combine local projects with container images built
                and published by other teams. Use a project reference for the service under
                change, and add another team's image as a container resource with a version or,
                preferably, an immutable digest. You do not need that team's source checkout to
                test its published contract, but you do need registry access and a reproducible
                image version.
            </p>
            <p>
                The AppHost can also start a real broker and the disposable dependencies its
                consumers need. Pass each container the broker endpoint and connection settings
                under the configuration names it already expects, and wait for the consumer to
                report that it is ready—not merely for the broker container to start. This is an
                optional enterprise extension; the companion article's anonymized production
                graph contains PostgreSQL and does not include Kafka.
            </p>
            <p>
                Test a cross-service contract by performing a UI action, correlating its unique
                entity or event identifier, and using bounded eventual assertions to wait for the
                consumer's downstream effect. Then reload or revisit the UI and assert the final
                user-visible state. Inspect a receiving API, database record, or message
                observation as supporting evidence when useful; broker health or enqueue success
                alone does not prove delivery or processing.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep event schemas compatible across the pinned service versions, isolate topics
                and consumer groups or queues per test run, and use synthetic data. Let the fixture
                own cleanup of its local resources; do not send test events to shared staging or
                production brokers.
            </p>

